<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> The humanities are best described as fields of study that are concerned with</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">technical training in making art.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">only the history of past civilizations.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">values: what human beings care about, fight over, and try to express.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">measurable facts about the natural world.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The humanities (philosophy, history, literature, the arts) share an interest in values, the things people care about and express. That is why we study them together.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about what separates the humanities from the sciences. The sciences measure and explain the natural world; the humanities study what people care about and how they express it.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Why does this course approach the humanities through the arts?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Because the arts avoid unpleasant subjects.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Because artists are better writers than historians or philosophers.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Because the arts are the only humanities that have survived from the ancient world.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Because artists reveal values: a painting shows what war's terror looks like instead of arguing that it is terrible.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Historians and philosophers study values; artists give them a form you can see, hear, or feel. That difference is the reason the course is built on the arts.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Look for the answer about what the arts do differently. Other humanities discuss values; the arts make them visible, audible, or tangible.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> David Alfaro Siqueiros's Echo of a Scream is hard to look at. What does that suggest about the subject matter of art?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">A painting that upsets the viewer has failed.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Art is not limited to the beautiful; disturbing works can reveal important values such as the human cost of war.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Only beautiful subjects can carry meaning.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Disturbing works cannot be considered real art.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The distortion and scale of the painting are exactly what force you to face its subject. Beauty is one value art can reveal, but not the only one.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Consider why the painting looks the way it does. Its discomfort is not a flaw; it is how the painting makes you confront the human cost of war.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Which statement best reflects this unit's view of taste and popularity?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">A work's commercial success does not by itself show that it is excellent.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Taste cannot be developed; you either like something or you do not.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Taste has nothing to do with values.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">The most popular works are always the best ones.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A hit may satisfy us for a while, but a work that examines human experience in a lasting way satisfies at a deeper level. Our ability to respond to art can also grow.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson argues that public success is not the same as excellence, and that taste can develop. Look for the answer that keeps those two ideas.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Peter Blume's The Eternal City seems strange and even silly at first glance. What changes when you learn that the green head is Mussolini?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">The painting becomes less meaningful because it is only about politics.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">You now feel something you did not feel before, because knowledge creates emotions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Your response deepens: the painting becomes a political protest, and you understand why it affects you.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Nothing changes, because background knowledge is irrelevant to art.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Background knowledge does not replace direct experience; it gives that experience something to work with and helps you understand why you feel what you feel.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson's point is that knowledge and experience work together. Knowing the background does not erase your first reaction; it helps explain and deepen it.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> In this course, artistic form means</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">the physical size of a work.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">a structure that makes the subject matter more meaningful.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">any arrangement of parts, however random.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">the artist's biography.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Every work has some structure, but artistic form is structure that does work: it focuses attention and reveals something about the subject.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Not every arrangement counts. A random pile of shapes has structure, but artistic form is structure that clarifies or intensifies what a work is about.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> The Mona Lisa is calm and balanced, while Echo of a Scream is jagged and fragmented. What does comparing them show?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Form has no effect on meaning.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Both have artistic form, but their forms do different things: one creates stillness and mystery, the other urgency and pain.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Only the Mona Lisa has artistic form, because it is calm.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">The two works have the same structure and differ only in subject.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Leonardo's form creates stillness; Siqueiros's creates urgency. In each, the structure of the painting is inseparable from what it means.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Both paintings are organized in some way, so both have form. The question is what each form does to its subject.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> What is the difference between looking at a work of art and perceiving it?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Looking is for paintings and perceiving is for music.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">There is no difference; the words mean the same thing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Perceiving means deciding whether you like the work.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Looking is passive, while perceiving is active: you notice how the parts relate and connect what you see to what you know.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Perception is attention in action: noticing relationships, registering composition, and connecting what you see to what you know.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson draws a line between passively taking in an image and actively noticing how its parts relate. Find the answer that makes perception the active one.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> Which of the following is an example of a concrete image giving form to an abstract idea?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">A wilting flower standing for mortality.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">A list of dates in an artist's life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">A dictionary definition of justice.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">A sentence stating that grief is painful.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Mortality is an abstract idea you can think about but not touch; a wilting flower is specific and physical. The arts work by giving abstract ideas concrete forms like this.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A concrete image is something specific and physical that stands for an idea. Look for the answer that shows a thing, not a statement about a thing.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> Dorothea Lange's Migrant Mother is a good test of the claim that background knowledge deepens a response. How?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Knowing it shows a family during the Great Depression gives the photograph's composition a subject matter that clarifies values such as dignity and society's obligations.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Knowing the background makes the composition disappear.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">The photograph has meaning only if you already know the family's names.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The context matters only to historians, not to viewers.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The composition was always there, but the context gives it a subject matter. You see the mother's gaze and the turned-away faces as part of a larger story about poverty and dignity.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson's argument is that structure and context work together. Context does not replace what you see; it gives the structure a subject to clarify.</div>
  </div>
</div>

